<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="D-myo-inositol-5-phosphate metabolism" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6367" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: MYO-INOSITOL &quot;&lt;i&gt;Myo&lt;/i&gt;-Inositol&quot;|-containing phospholipids play an important role in the control of cellular functions within eukaryotic cells, being part of the intracellular signal transductions pathways from various regulators, including hormones, neurotransmitters, growth factors, and serum |CITS: [164246]|. When these regulators interact with their receptors in cell membranes, they induce the hydrolysis of certain |FRAME: Phosphoinositides &quot;phosphoinositides&quot;| to produce second messenger molecules. |FRAME: 1-PHOSPHATIDYL-1D-MYO-INOSITOL-5-PHOSPHA &quot;Phosphatidylinositol 5-phosphate&quot;| was discovered in 1997 in mammalian fibroblasts as a source for |FRAME: PHOSPHATIDYL-MYO-INOSITOL-45-BISPHOSPHA &quot;phosphatidylinositol 4,5-bisphosphate&quot;| |CITS: [9367159]|, and is still considered the least-characterized member of the phosphoinositol family. It represents only 0.5% of the PI pool present in the cells, but its levels strongly fluctuate due to external stimuli such as TCR activation, insulin treatment, oxidative stress, and pathogen cellular invasion |CITS: [28302928]|. It is believed to play a role as a regulator of membrane trafficking |CITS: [18774718]|, and is considered as crucial for the endocytic pathway, intracellular signaling, and adaptation to stress |CITS: [28302928]|. The best established pathway for the synthesis of |FRAME: 1-PHOSPHATIDYL-1D-MYO-INOSITOL-5-PHOSPHA &quot;phosphatidylinositol 5-phosphate&quot;| is the dephosphorylation of |FRAME: 1-PHOSPHATIDYL-1D-MYO-INOSITOL-35-BISPH|, catalyzed by a number of myotubularin-related phosphatases, such as |FRAME: HS02045| |CITS: [11676921]| and |FRAME: HS08920| |CITS: [17008356]|. Another source for the compound is the removal of the 4-phosphate group from |FRAME: PHOSPHATIDYL-MYO-INOSITOL-45-BISPHOSPHA| by either of two dedicated phsphatases, encoded by |FRAME: HS14553| and |FRAME: HS15363| |CITS: [16365287]|. It has been shown that |FRAME: 1-PHOSPHATIDYL-1D-MYO-INOSITOL-5-PHOSPHA &quot;phosphatidylinositol 5-phosphate&quot;| is phosphorylated to |FRAME: PHOSPHATIDYL-MYO-INOSITOL-45-BISPHOSPHA| by |FRAME: HS07693-MONOMER|, encoded by |FRAME: HS07693| |CITS: [9367159]|. However, the concentration of |FRAME: 1-PHOSPHATIDYL-1D-MYO-INOSITOL-5-PHOSPHA &quot;phosphatidylinositol 5-phosphate&quot;| in the cell is very low (about 2% of |FRAME: CPD-1108 &quot;phosphatidylinositol 4-phosphate&quot;|), and thus it is unlikely that its main role is as a source of |FRAME: PHOSPHATIDYL-MYO-INOSITOL-45-BISPHOSPHA| biosynthesis. However, the exact role of this compound in the cell is still unknown.</Description>
